00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:46.480
Without further ado, let's get
to answering your questions.

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:49.650
The first question is
about task switching.

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:52.170
The specific
question is, is there

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:55.440
a way to get better
at task switching?

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:58.870
Task switching is an
incredibly interesting topic.

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:01.440
It's something that
plagues many people.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:04.290
That is, a lot of people have
challenges with task switching.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:07.860
It's also a topic that
people will often confuse

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.919
with cognitive flexibility.

00:02:10.919 --> 00:02:13.440
All of us, unless
it's been removed,

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.350
have an area of our brain
called the prefrontal cortex.

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:18.120
The words prefrontal
cortex actually

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:22.440
refers to a fairly varied real
estate within the human brain.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:24.420
It's not one area
of the human brain.

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:26.580
Prefrontal cortex
actually includes a lot

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.980
of different subdivisions
that do different things

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:32.250
in the context of cognition
and directing action,

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:34.637
withholding action,
these kinds of things.

00:02:34.637 --> 00:02:36.720
One of the main functions
of the prefrontal cortex

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:39.180
is that when it's
working well, it

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:42.880
allows us to direct our
focus and our cognition,

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:47.580
our thinking, in a
context-dependent way.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.590
One of the simplest
ways to describe this

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:52.050
is that when you took
math in high school

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:53.820
or if you're still
taking math, your brain

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:56.070
had to carry out certain
types of cognitive operations

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:57.888
that were very
different than the types

00:02:57.888 --> 00:02:59.430
of cognitive operations
that you need

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:02.400
to carry out in your history
class or your social studies

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:03.810
class.

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:05.760
There were some
features of all three

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:08.370
of those classes
that were the same,

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:10.070
in the sense that
presumably, you

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:12.390
had to sit in a chair
for all of those classes.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:13.958
You followed a
certain set of rules

00:03:13.958 --> 00:03:16.250
that pertained to all three
of those different classes,

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:17.360
even though there are
different subjects.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:19.820
But then, there were certain
rules that pertained just

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:22.220
to mathematics, certain
rules that you followed

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:24.770
because a particular teacher
was strict-- not because

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:26.150
of the topic they
were covering--

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:28.862
as well as certain rules that
maybe you did not pay attention

00:03:28.862 --> 00:03:31.070
to because a different
teacher was a little more lax.

00:03:31.070 --> 00:03:32.180
For instance, maybe
there was a teacher

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:34.638
that let you put your feet up
on the chair in front of you.

00:03:34.638 --> 00:03:37.310
Maybe, another teacher
forbid that at all costs.

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:39.320
The point being that
your prefrontal cortex

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:42.830
is the area of your brain
that, along with other areas

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:44.960
of your brain, ensures
that you engage

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:48.950
in context-specific behavior,
context-specific thinking

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:53.120
and context-specific
understanding about what

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:54.830
you should and should not do.

00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:57.080
Now, cognitive
flexibility is similar,

00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:01.190
in the sense that it describes
your ability to switch

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:03.140
the types of
cognitive operations--

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:04.670
as the name suggests--

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:06.650
depending on what
sorts of things

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:08.400
you're trying to
learn or understand.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:09.900
It's a lot more
extensive than that.

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:11.817
In fact, we will probably
do an entire episode

00:04:11.817 --> 00:04:14.250
all about both cognitive
flexibility and perhaps,

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:16.470
even a separate episode
on task switching.

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:18.120
Task switching is
somewhat distinct

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:19.410
from cognitive flexibility.

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:23.070
First of all task, switching
requires cognitive flexibility,

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:25.000
but they are not the same thing.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:27.943
Now, when we talk about
task switching-- or rather,

00:04:27.943 --> 00:04:30.360
when you see task switching
in the scientific literature--

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:33.150
most often, it has
to do with people

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:37.140
performing one particular
type of mental or physical

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:38.820
operation.

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:41.550
Say, they're maneuvering
things with their hands

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:44.130
or other parts of
their body, or they

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:46.440
are required to carry
out one specific type

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:48.360
of mental process.

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:52.350
And then, they are required,
either at random intervals

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:54.570
or at specific intervals--
maybe every 10 minutes

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:57.030
or so-- to switch
their attention

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:59.730
and to do a different
task entirely.

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:02.160
In the laboratory
experiment situation,

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:04.680
this has most typically been
carried out the following way.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:08.250
People are going to do
one cognitive task, maybe

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:13.410
mathematics, or they're going
to count, for instance, from 1

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:16.290
up to infinity, as
high as they can

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:19.950
go in a given amount of
time, in increments of say,

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:24.870
7 or increments of 7
plus 1 then 7 minus 1.

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:27.340
These can be made increasingly
difficult. You get the idea.

00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:29.373
And then perhaps,
a tone is played

00:05:29.373 --> 00:05:31.290
or they'll get a signal
from the experimenter.

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:34.110
They need to switch their
task to doing something quite

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:35.640
different, but also cognitive.

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:38.280
That's the most
typical arrangement.

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:41.070
Another typical arrangement
in a task switching experiment

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:43.950
is that the person in the
task switching experiment

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:47.130
will be asked to do some
sort of physical manipulation

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:49.800
of objects, maybe
placement of puzzle pieces

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:51.840
into the correct configuration.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:55.500
Then at some designated
interval or intervals,

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:59.400
they will have to switch
to a different manual task.

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:02.370
Fewer-- not zero, but
fewer-- experiments

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:04.380
have examined task
switching between

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:08.448
physical and cognitive tasks.

00:06:08.448 --> 00:06:10.240
There are these kind
of outrageous examples

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:11.657
that you can find
on the internet.

00:06:11.657 --> 00:06:13.420
By the way, I don't
suggest that anyone

00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:17.090
go engage in these examples
in real life of extreme task

00:06:17.090 --> 00:06:17.590
switching.

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:20.380
One of the most notable
ones would be chess boxing.

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:22.150
Believe it or not,
this exists, where

00:06:22.150 --> 00:06:23.980
two people will enter a ring.

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:26.380
They will sit down at a table.

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:30.370
They will play chess for
a given period of time.

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:32.440
They're entirely focused
on playing chess.

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:33.687
Then a buzzer will go off.

00:06:33.687 --> 00:06:35.020
The chess table will be cleared.

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:36.145
The chairs will be cleared.

00:06:36.145 --> 00:06:38.920
They will be expected
to box, literally

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:42.850
fight, for a round of a
minute to three minutes,

00:06:42.850 --> 00:06:45.860
and then, go back to chess,
then to boxing, so-called chess

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:46.360
boxing.

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:48.850
Again, I'm not suggesting
people chess box.

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:53.440
But I know that many people have
challenges with task switching.

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:56.500
Here, I can raise my hand and
say that I am one such person.

00:06:56.500 --> 00:07:01.330
I've always had a pretty good
ability to drop into deep focus

00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:02.888
after a period of time.

00:07:02.888 --> 00:07:04.930
I, like everybody else,
take a little bit of time

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:07.630
to get into a book
chapter or to get

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:09.550
into a mode of
physical exercise.

00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:11.140
But once I'm doing
something, I tend

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:12.880
to be very focused on that.

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:15.520
I have a much greater
challenge in switching out

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:17.800
of that focused mode to
doing the next thing, which

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:20.110
is one of the reasons why
oftentimes, I run tardy,

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:23.590
because I'm still mentally
thinking about or physically

00:07:23.590 --> 00:07:26.110
engaged in the thing
that I was doing before.

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:27.970
This is something I'm
constantly working on.

00:07:27.970 --> 00:07:30.137
As a consequence, I've had
to seek out and implement

00:07:30.137 --> 00:07:32.360
certain tools to improve
my ability to task switch.

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:34.360
I'm going to share a few
of those tools with you

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:36.520
now, because I know
a number of people

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:39.790
probably struggle
with the same thing.

00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:41.410
As I mentioned
earlier, I'm also going

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:43.720
to do a full-length episode
about task switching,

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:46.720
both the underlying mechanisms
of task switching as well

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:48.580
as a more extensive
list of tools related

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:50.770
to task switching, as a
full-length Huberman Lab

00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:52.270
podcast episode.

00:07:52.270 --> 00:07:54.130
How can we get better
at task switching?

00:07:54.130 --> 00:07:56.290
Short of having somebody
scruff you by the neck

00:07:56.290 --> 00:07:58.720
and force you to stop
whatever activity you're doing

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:01.383
and engage in the next
activity that you're doing,

00:08:01.383 --> 00:08:02.800
one of the best
things that we can

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:06.220
do to support our ability to
task switch-- that's nicely

00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:08.440
supported both at
the mechanistic level

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:11.350
and at the practical
level within the published

00:08:11.350 --> 00:08:14.920
literature-- is to
introduce short transition

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:18.700
gaps between the activities that
we're trying to switch between.

00:08:18.700 --> 00:08:20.330
This is something
that, in my opinion,

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:21.860
has not been discussed enough.

00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:24.820
In fact, when was the last time
you heard about the requirement

00:08:24.820 --> 00:08:27.040
for introducing
gaps between tasks

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:29.230
if you want to switch between
them more efficiently?

00:08:29.230 --> 00:08:30.640
And yet, as a
consequence of this

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:33.520
not being discussed very
often, I think a lot of people

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:36.020
have placed an undue
burden on themselves.

00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:38.049
For instance, a lot of
people think that when

00:08:38.049 --> 00:08:40.360
you sit down with a book
and you're going to read,

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:44.122
that you should be able
to immediately focus

00:08:44.122 --> 00:08:45.580
on the material
that you're reading

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:47.680
and not have your
mind flitting about.

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:50.740
During the first five,
maybe even 10 minutes

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:54.370
of reading a book chapter,
unless you are absolutely

00:08:54.370 --> 00:08:57.790
enthralled from the
first word, or you

00:08:57.790 --> 00:09:02.440
are intensely curious what the
material in that book chapter

00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:03.130
is--

00:09:03.130 --> 00:09:04.630
maybe that book
chapter is about you

00:09:04.630 --> 00:09:06.340
and what's going to happen
to you next in your life.

00:09:06.340 --> 00:09:08.230
Maybe the news article
is about something

00:09:08.230 --> 00:09:09.820
that you care oh so much about.

00:09:09.820 --> 00:09:13.670
But unless it's one of
those specific instances,

00:09:13.670 --> 00:09:15.273
it's going to be
about five or 10

00:09:15.273 --> 00:09:16.690
minutes before the
neural circuits

00:09:16.690 --> 00:09:19.360
in your brain that are
required to understand

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:22.030
and digest and commit
that material to memory

00:09:22.030 --> 00:09:25.330
are going to come online at
the levels of activity that

00:09:25.330 --> 00:09:28.390
are going to be required
for you to experience

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:32.500
that as intense focus,
or even as mild focus.

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:34.360
The activity of
the brain is always

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:35.830
going to be in a push-pull.

00:09:35.830 --> 00:09:38.410
This is extremely important for
understanding task switching.

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:40.690
When you go from one task--

00:09:40.690 --> 00:09:42.550
and maybe the task was
simply to walk over

00:09:42.550 --> 00:09:43.930
to where the book is located--

00:09:43.930 --> 00:09:47.050
to focusing on the
material within that book,

00:09:47.050 --> 00:09:51.370
you have to both engage activity
within certain neural circuits

00:09:51.370 --> 00:09:54.160
and you need to
disengage the activity

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:55.930
of other neural circuits.

00:09:55.930 --> 00:09:58.375
Sometimes, this is
referred to as inhibition

00:09:58.375 --> 00:09:59.500
of certain neural circuits.

00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:01.960
Other times, it's just going
to be a dissipation of activity

00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:02.680
of those neural circuits.

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:04.180
They're just going
to quiet down,

00:10:04.180 --> 00:10:06.460
like a dimming of the
lights in a particular room,

00:10:06.460 --> 00:10:10.295
while the activity of other
neural circuits increases.

00:10:10.295 --> 00:10:12.420
The first thing that you
really need to understand,

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:14.253
if you want to get
better at task switching,

00:10:14.253 --> 00:10:16.980
is that you cannot and you
should not expect yourself

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:20.220
to immediately drop into
a narrow trench of focus,

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:23.970
or a narrow trench of ability,
for anything that you're not

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:28.200
already extremely skilled
at or extremely interested

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:29.640
in knowing.

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:31.710
One of the reasons why
this is often overlooked

00:10:31.710 --> 00:10:35.370
is that, for instance,
if we receive a text

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:38.520
message from somebody and we
are very interested in what's

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:41.940
contained in that text message,
maybe even eagerly anticipating

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:44.025
the dot, dot, dot in
that little window

00:10:44.025 --> 00:10:46.650
where the text message is going
to arrive-- like here it comes.

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:47.010
Here it comes.

00:10:47.010 --> 00:10:47.593
Here it comes.

00:10:47.593 --> 00:10:51.960
It's an example of where you
are able to immediately pay

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:53.730
attention and
absorb information.

00:10:53.730 --> 00:10:56.550
For instance, if you're trying
to meet somebody in a big city,

00:10:56.550 --> 00:10:57.870
and you need to know
exactly where to meet them,

00:10:57.870 --> 00:10:59.130
and you've arrived
at the place where

00:10:59.130 --> 00:11:01.020
you thought you need to be
and then, you can't find them.

00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:01.680
You're waiting.

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:02.263
Where are you?

00:11:02.263 --> 00:11:03.150
Where are you?

00:11:03.150 --> 00:11:04.780
You're going to commit
that information to memory

00:11:04.780 --> 00:11:06.030
and you're going to act on it.

00:11:06.030 --> 00:11:08.880
But when you sit down to read
a book of unknown content,

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:11.520
or where you have just a general
sense of what the content is,

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:13.920
or when you sit down to
do something like work

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:16.380
on a spreadsheet or
your taxes, or engage

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:19.350
in a conversation with somebody,
expect a five to 10 minute

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:20.190
transition period.

00:11:20.190 --> 00:11:23.430
I can't emphasize this enough,
because I think a lot of people

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:27.330
mistakenly think that they
have issues with attention.

00:11:27.330 --> 00:11:29.940
Perhaps indeed, they have
clinically diagnosable

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:32.970
attention deficit
hyperactivity disorder

00:11:32.970 --> 00:11:35.580
or some other form of
attention deficit disorder.

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:38.130
I certainly can't rule that
out based on this conversation

00:11:38.130 --> 00:11:38.700
alone.

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:43.230
But a lot of people place this
unfair burden on themselves

00:11:43.230 --> 00:11:46.500
to immediately be able
to focus on a given task.

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:48.940
This is also true
for physical tasks.

00:11:48.940 --> 00:11:52.720
If you go to the gym to work
out or you're heading out

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:55.720
on a run or a
cycling expedition,

00:11:55.720 --> 00:11:59.410
the idea that you would
immediately be able to cycle

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:02.470
at your peak performance, or
that you could perform sets

00:12:02.470 --> 00:12:04.630
and reps in the gym as
best as you possibly

00:12:04.630 --> 00:12:07.480
could without any warm-up,
without any transition period,

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:10.240
that you could forget about the
difficult or maybe even great

00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:12.430
conversation that you
were having on the way in,

00:12:12.430 --> 00:12:14.530
or that you could forget
about other activities

00:12:14.530 --> 00:12:16.870
that you need to do in
the rest of your day,

00:12:16.870 --> 00:12:18.640
that's just completely unfair.

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:22.750
It doesn't match at all the way
that your neural circuits work.

00:12:22.750 --> 00:12:26.560
You really need to match your
expectation of your ability

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:28.390
to focus on and
perform a given task,

00:12:28.390 --> 00:12:30.400
whether or not it's
cognitive or physical, to

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:32.260
the actual underlying biology.

00:12:32.260 --> 00:12:33.350
That's the first point.

00:12:33.350 --> 00:12:38.860
The second point is that we know
that if you want to switch from

00:12:38.860 --> 00:12:43.390
one task to another task,
that you are making it more

00:12:43.390 --> 00:12:48.070
difficult to drop into full-task
engagement-- or rather,

00:12:48.070 --> 00:12:52.300
engagement with task
B following task A,

00:12:52.300 --> 00:12:55.410
if you try and go immediately
from task A to task B--

00:12:55.410 --> 00:12:57.160
that even the introduction--

00:12:57.160 --> 00:12:59.710
I find this so cool--
even the introduction

00:12:59.710 --> 00:13:04.210
of an arbitrary but very short
transition period of say,

00:13:04.210 --> 00:13:07.240
15 seconds, where you know
that you're introducing

00:13:07.240 --> 00:13:11.470
15 seconds of transition and
you designate it as transition,

00:13:11.470 --> 00:13:13.930
will allow you to engage in
a more efficient and more

00:13:13.930 --> 00:13:18.670
complete level of task
execution on task B

00:13:18.670 --> 00:13:22.810
if you introduce even a
brief transition period.

00:13:22.810 --> 00:13:25.870
This I find fascinating,
because what this means

00:13:25.870 --> 00:13:28.120
is that there are
top down influences.

00:13:28.120 --> 00:13:30.370
There are literally things
that we can tell ourselves,

00:13:30.370 --> 00:13:33.730
based on an understanding of
the underlying mechanisms, that

00:13:33.730 --> 00:13:35.620
allow us to task switch better.

00:13:35.620 --> 00:13:38.290
This certainly doesn't involve
taking any kind of prescription

00:13:38.290 --> 00:13:40.690
drug or supplement or
doing anything differently,

00:13:40.690 --> 00:13:44.950
except as you go from
task A to task B,

00:13:44.950 --> 00:13:49.690
knowing and designating that a
transition period, even a very

00:13:49.690 --> 00:13:53.050
brief one where you are not
trying to perform task B

00:13:53.050 --> 00:13:55.780
and that you've designated
this as a transition period.

00:13:55.780 --> 00:13:59.950
I'm not trying to focus on the
next thing that I need to do.

00:13:59.950 --> 00:14:01.450
I might focus on
it inadvertently,

00:14:01.450 --> 00:14:05.250
but I'm not deliberately
trying to focus on it.

00:14:05.250 --> 00:14:07.970
Rather, I'm going to think
about what I just did

00:14:07.970 --> 00:14:10.820
and the fact that I'm no longer
doing that, kind of leaving it

00:14:10.820 --> 00:14:12.410
like a fog behind.

00:14:12.410 --> 00:14:15.170
You're trying to move from
this deep trench of attention,

00:14:15.170 --> 00:14:16.730
hopefully on task
A-- or maybe, you

00:14:16.730 --> 00:14:18.980
didn't achieve a deep
trench of attention--

00:14:18.980 --> 00:14:22.730
and you're now done
with task A. You're not

00:14:22.730 --> 00:14:25.460
placing this unfair expectation
on your neural circuits

00:14:25.460 --> 00:14:29.390
to just flip to task B.
You're also acknowledging

00:14:29.390 --> 00:14:32.420
that task B is going to take
five to 10 minutes to drop

00:14:32.420 --> 00:14:32.960
into fully.

00:14:32.960 --> 00:14:34.168
We already talked about that.

00:14:34.168 --> 00:14:36.800
But you're going to shorten
that five to 10 minutes

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:40.760
by deliberately introducing
a transition period.

00:14:40.760 --> 00:14:43.490
What comes in that transition
period and its duration

00:14:43.490 --> 00:14:44.690
is important.

00:14:44.690 --> 00:14:46.790
First, let's deal
with the duration.

00:14:46.790 --> 00:14:49.770
How long should the
transition period be?

00:14:49.770 --> 00:14:53.060
That is going to scale
directly with how long you

00:14:53.060 --> 00:14:55.912
are in a deep trench of
focus for task A. Let's

00:14:55.912 --> 00:14:58.370
assume task A was something
that was kind of light for you.

00:14:58.370 --> 00:14:59.995
Maybe, you're just
handling some email.

00:14:59.995 --> 00:15:01.910
Maybe, you're talking
to a coworker.

00:15:01.910 --> 00:15:03.848
Maybe, you were
at a board meeting

00:15:03.848 --> 00:15:04.890
and it was kind of light.

00:15:04.890 --> 00:15:06.067
The stuff was just OK.

00:15:06.067 --> 00:15:07.150
You're used to this stuff.

00:15:07.150 --> 00:15:08.550
This is stuff that
you do all the time.

00:15:08.550 --> 00:15:10.230
Now, you're headed
back to your desk

00:15:10.230 --> 00:15:11.910
or you're headed
to your next class.

00:15:11.910 --> 00:15:14.070
Or perhaps, you did a
workout that morning

00:15:14.070 --> 00:15:17.293
and now, you're going to
head to your place of work.

00:15:17.293 --> 00:15:18.960
Or maybe, you're
leaving work and you're

00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:21.780
going to engage with family.

00:15:21.780 --> 00:15:24.315
You know need to switch all
these cognitive operations.

00:15:24.315 --> 00:15:26.190
You need to dump the
stuff that you were just

00:15:26.190 --> 00:15:28.200
doing cognitively,
and you now need

00:15:28.200 --> 00:15:29.520
to do a bunch of other things.

00:15:29.520 --> 00:15:31.170
Context is switching.

00:15:31.170 --> 00:15:33.720
Task is switching.

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:36.420
Just ask yourself,
how deeply was I

00:15:36.420 --> 00:15:37.890
entrenched in that
other activity?

00:15:37.890 --> 00:15:40.170
Was my mind flitting
to other things?

00:15:40.170 --> 00:15:44.850
If I was in a deep trench of
attention for that given thing,

00:15:44.850 --> 00:15:47.070
then you should give
yourself slightly longer

00:15:47.070 --> 00:15:50.310
for this transition period,
maybe five or even 10 minutes

00:15:50.310 --> 00:15:52.110
if you have that time.

00:15:52.110 --> 00:15:55.020
Even if you give yourself
as short as 60 to 90 seconds

00:15:55.020 --> 00:15:58.540
of transition and you just
designate it as transition,

00:15:58.540 --> 00:16:00.540
you're going to benefit
in terms of your ability

00:16:00.540 --> 00:16:02.010
to do the next task.

00:16:02.010 --> 00:16:04.830
To be very clear, if you
were in kind of a light task

00:16:04.830 --> 00:16:07.620
or something that didn't
have much cognitive demand,

00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:09.900
then the transition period
can be fairly short.

00:16:09.900 --> 00:16:12.030
It can be just a
couple of minutes.

00:16:12.030 --> 00:16:14.580
Rather, if you were in a
deep trench of attention,

00:16:14.580 --> 00:16:17.310
you really engaged
in that first task,

00:16:17.310 --> 00:16:18.930
I suggest giving
yourself a couple

00:16:18.930 --> 00:16:21.487
of minutes or more, maybe as
much as five to 10 minutes.

00:16:21.487 --> 00:16:23.070
But you might not
have that much time.

00:16:23.070 --> 00:16:25.440
In which case, give yourself
any kind of transition,

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:27.060
even if it's 10 seconds.

00:16:27.060 --> 00:16:29.190
I certainly have had
times in my life,

00:16:29.190 --> 00:16:32.640
in particular when I was a new
assistant professor before I

00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:34.788
got tenure, where I
remember sitting down

00:16:34.788 --> 00:16:35.580
to work on a grant.

00:16:35.580 --> 00:16:36.497
I'd get two lines out.

00:16:36.497 --> 00:16:37.860
Someone would knock on the door.

00:16:37.860 --> 00:16:40.530
Hey, where are the whatever,
the 30 mil syringes?

00:16:40.530 --> 00:16:42.270
Where do we keep the buffers?

00:16:42.270 --> 00:16:44.413
Did we get this thing in?

00:16:44.413 --> 00:16:46.080
And then, I'd have
to shift my attention

00:16:46.080 --> 00:16:47.038
and go back to writing.

00:16:47.038 --> 00:16:49.238
I'd be distracted by
something else again.

00:16:49.238 --> 00:16:51.780
Which is not to say that people
were distracting me unfairly.

00:16:51.780 --> 00:16:54.150
It was simply the case
that, at that time,

00:16:54.150 --> 00:16:57.330
my life required being
involved in a lot more things

00:16:57.330 --> 00:16:59.100
than it did as my
career progressed,

00:16:59.100 --> 00:17:02.070
at least in the short term.

00:17:02.070 --> 00:17:04.813
The point being that if you are
deeply engaged in an activity,

00:17:04.813 --> 00:17:07.230
give yourself a little bit
longer in the transition period

00:17:07.230 --> 00:17:07.920
between them.

00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:11.458
If you are sort of superficially
involved in an activity,

00:17:11.458 --> 00:17:13.000
you need less of a
transition period.

00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:14.550
But you need a
transition period.

00:17:14.550 --> 00:17:17.520
What should come during
that transition period?

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:19.859
The most important thing to
arrive in that transition

00:17:19.859 --> 00:17:24.220
period is a relative lack of
attention to anything new.

00:17:24.220 --> 00:17:26.579
This is what's so
destructive about the phone.

00:17:26.579 --> 00:17:29.130
Keep in mind, I am not one
of these people that thinks

00:17:29.130 --> 00:17:30.690
that smartphones are terrible.

00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:36.660
In fact, I use mine plural,
very often, all day, often.

00:17:36.660 --> 00:17:38.400
Not necessarily
during deep cognitive

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:40.860
focus, but in between
those bouts of focus,

00:17:40.860 --> 00:17:42.210
I have to text message people.

00:17:42.210 --> 00:17:43.530
I do work on there.

00:17:43.530 --> 00:17:45.420
I'm on social
media, so certainly

00:17:45.420 --> 00:17:47.550
not demonizing the smartphone.

00:17:47.550 --> 00:17:50.463
However, if you finish
a given activity--

00:17:50.463 --> 00:17:52.630
whether or not it's cognitive
or physical activity--

00:17:52.630 --> 00:17:57.060
and you are headed to something
else that requires you do a new

00:17:57.060 --> 00:18:00.450
task, and that task
requires significant amounts

00:18:00.450 --> 00:18:04.080
of attention and focus, then
you would do very well to allow

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:07.110
yourself a period of anywhere
from two minutes to maybe,

00:18:07.110 --> 00:18:08.370
as long as 10 minutes--

00:18:08.370 --> 00:18:10.412
I know this is going to
be very hard for people--

00:18:10.412 --> 00:18:12.090
but two minutes to
as long as 10 minutes

00:18:12.090 --> 00:18:14.970
where you are not
looking at your phone.

00:18:14.970 --> 00:18:16.200
You're not texting.

00:18:16.200 --> 00:18:17.890
You're not on social media.

00:18:17.890 --> 00:18:19.435
You're not foraging
for anything.

00:18:19.435 --> 00:18:22.060
In fact, you're trying to limit
the total amount of information

00:18:22.060 --> 00:18:23.450
that you're bringing
into your nervous system.

00:18:23.450 --> 00:18:25.575
Now, you don't have to walk
around with eyes closed

00:18:25.575 --> 00:18:27.280
and try and not
hear and not see.

00:18:27.280 --> 00:18:28.450
Let's be practical, folks.

00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:29.980
That's impossible to do anyway.

00:18:29.980 --> 00:18:32.680
You can't shut down
your brain while awake.

00:18:32.680 --> 00:18:35.250
You can go into states
of deeper relaxation.

00:18:35.250 --> 00:18:37.000
There's a non-sleep
deep rest, which we'll

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:38.125
talk about in a little bit.

00:18:38.125 --> 00:18:41.540
But you can't shut off
your brain deliberately,

00:18:41.540 --> 00:18:43.190
not in any healthy way that is.

00:18:43.190 --> 00:18:47.720
But by introducing these
transition zones, or transition

00:18:47.720 --> 00:18:50.270
periods as we'll call
them, of two to 10 minutes

00:18:50.270 --> 00:18:52.130
between different
tasks, and making sure

00:18:52.130 --> 00:18:53.960
that within those
transition periods,

00:18:53.960 --> 00:18:57.620
you are not bringing in
new information-- again,

00:18:57.620 --> 00:18:59.930
another context and what
are you really doing?

00:18:59.930 --> 00:19:02.780
You're ensuring that you're
not going from task A to task B

00:19:02.780 --> 00:19:04.760
to task C.

00:19:04.760 --> 00:19:06.710
What we're talking
about here is trying

00:19:06.710 --> 00:19:10.250
to limit your task switching
between task A and task B

00:19:10.250 --> 00:19:12.650
and not introducing
another task in between.

00:19:12.650 --> 00:19:16.700
You might think that looking
at your phone is not a task.

00:19:16.700 --> 00:19:17.390
It's so easy.

00:19:17.390 --> 00:19:18.620
It's so reflexive.

00:19:18.620 --> 00:19:19.520
But it is.

00:19:19.520 --> 00:19:22.340
It's bringing in a
lot of new context,

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:24.170
in particular pictures
and movies which

00:19:24.170 --> 00:19:26.090
are tremendous stimulus
for the nervous system

00:19:26.090 --> 00:19:27.540
and anchoring your attention.

00:19:27.540 --> 00:19:29.630
It's bringing in new
ideas, new thoughts

00:19:29.630 --> 00:19:31.700
that no matter how
hard you try, are

00:19:31.700 --> 00:19:36.890
going to intrude into your
ability to perform task B. When

00:19:36.890 --> 00:19:39.640
people say, how do I get
better at task switching,

00:19:39.640 --> 00:19:41.410
I immediately want
to say, please

00:19:41.410 --> 00:19:43.420
don't introduce yet more tasks.

00:19:43.420 --> 00:19:46.330
Switching from one task to
another is hard enough already.

00:19:46.330 --> 00:19:48.640
Don't introduce another
task in between.

00:19:48.640 --> 00:19:50.477
Some of you might
take this to mean

00:19:50.477 --> 00:19:52.060
that you shouldn't
have a conversation

00:19:52.060 --> 00:19:54.640
with a coworker after a meeting
while walking down the hall.

00:19:54.640 --> 00:19:55.810
I'm not saying that.

00:19:55.810 --> 00:19:58.270
I still encourage
people to be social.

00:19:58.270 --> 00:20:00.920
I encourage people to engage
in workplace environments.

00:20:00.920 --> 00:20:03.315
However, I will say,
after many years

00:20:03.315 --> 00:20:05.440
of working in laboratories
that at times were quite

00:20:05.440 --> 00:20:07.395
large, if you walk
into the lab and there

00:20:07.395 --> 00:20:09.020
are a lot of different
things going on,

00:20:09.020 --> 00:20:10.812
one of the things that
you learn how to do,

00:20:10.812 --> 00:20:12.940
if you're going to get
good at your craft,

00:20:12.940 --> 00:20:15.310
is to not pay
attention to what's

00:20:15.310 --> 00:20:17.950
going on with everyone crowded
around a computer looking

00:20:17.950 --> 00:20:19.570
at who's winning
at the World Cup.

00:20:19.570 --> 00:20:21.445
I'm not trying to insult
soccer players here.

00:20:21.445 --> 00:20:23.560
I enjoy soccer, both
playing it and observing it.

00:20:23.560 --> 00:20:28.420
But one has to scruff themselves
a little bit in trying

00:20:28.420 --> 00:20:31.180
to limit their attention to
a number of different things

00:20:31.180 --> 00:20:33.865
in the environment, and
really go from task A

00:20:33.865 --> 00:20:36.580
to task B in a
really dedicated way.

00:20:36.580 --> 00:20:38.568
Short lists benefit
certain people.

00:20:38.568 --> 00:20:40.610
I know a lot of people
are list makers out there.

00:20:40.610 --> 00:20:43.610
They like to put two or three
things, or maybe 20 things

00:20:43.610 --> 00:20:45.440
that they're going to
accomplish each day.

00:20:45.440 --> 00:20:47.090
One of the best
tools that I ever

00:20:47.090 --> 00:20:49.340
learned, both for the
sake of task switching

00:20:49.340 --> 00:20:51.350
but also for sake of
just getting things

00:20:51.350 --> 00:20:52.960
done on a consistent basis--

00:20:52.960 --> 00:20:54.710
I picked up while I
was a master's student

00:20:54.710 --> 00:20:57.140
at Berkeley-- a very
accomplished professor

00:20:57.140 --> 00:21:00.440
at that time told me that he
writes down every day three

00:21:00.440 --> 00:21:01.940
things that he's
going to accomplish

00:21:01.940 --> 00:21:05.330
and only three things,
never more than three.

00:21:05.330 --> 00:21:06.960
He also included
other activities.

00:21:06.960 --> 00:21:10.280
In fact, he was quite
active in his physical life.

00:21:10.280 --> 00:21:11.960
He rode his bike to campus.

00:21:11.960 --> 00:21:13.190
He also was a runner.

00:21:13.190 --> 00:21:14.240
He also went to the gym.

00:21:14.240 --> 00:21:16.448
He did not include those on
his list of three things.

00:21:16.448 --> 00:21:19.790
He would write down no more
than three critical things

00:21:19.790 --> 00:21:20.480
to do each day.

00:21:20.480 --> 00:21:23.510
He had three critical tasks.

00:21:23.510 --> 00:21:25.040
I've employed that
method as well.

00:21:25.040 --> 00:21:28.280
I'll write down one, sometimes
two, most often three,

00:21:28.280 --> 00:21:31.160
but if I can, just one
or two tasks that I

00:21:31.160 --> 00:21:32.840
need to complete each day.

00:21:32.840 --> 00:21:34.790
Everything else is
considered part of the,

00:21:34.790 --> 00:21:37.200
let's just say,
automaticity function

00:21:37.200 --> 00:21:39.200
of my day, things that I
already know how to do.

00:21:39.200 --> 00:21:41.270
They don't require a
ton of cognitive focus.

00:21:41.270 --> 00:21:42.950
But I limit the
things that require

00:21:42.950 --> 00:21:45.212
a lot of cognitive focus
to three things per day.

00:21:45.212 --> 00:21:46.670
However, those
three things per day

00:21:46.670 --> 00:21:48.440
can take up many,
many hours each

00:21:48.440 --> 00:21:50.030
and certainly, on the whole.

00:21:50.030 --> 00:21:51.530
There are additional
things that one

00:21:51.530 --> 00:21:53.720
can do to improve your
ability to task switch.

00:21:53.720 --> 00:21:56.000
One of the things that I
found particularly beneficial

00:21:56.000 --> 00:22:00.250
is not a meditation, but rather,
is a perceptual exercise.

00:22:00.250 --> 00:22:02.120
This is a perceptual
exercise that I

00:22:02.120 --> 00:22:04.280
learned about when I
was a graduate student,

00:22:04.280 --> 00:22:05.960
but in a totally
different context.

00:22:05.960 --> 00:22:08.510
It has to do with the way
that your visual system

00:22:08.510 --> 00:22:12.110
and the parts of your
brain that parse time

00:22:12.110 --> 00:22:14.932
are related to one another
and influence one another.

00:22:14.932 --> 00:22:17.390
The reason this tool makes
sense for improving your ability

00:22:17.390 --> 00:22:20.210
to task switch is
because it turns out

00:22:20.210 --> 00:22:22.820
that where you focus your
visual attention strongly

00:22:22.820 --> 00:22:27.672
influences the way that
your brain parses time.

00:22:27.672 --> 00:22:28.880
I'll describe the tool first.

00:22:28.880 --> 00:22:31.505
And then, I'll get a little bit
into the underlying mechanisms.

00:22:31.505 --> 00:22:34.732
Again, I'll get deep into the
underlying mechanisms, as well

00:22:34.732 --> 00:22:36.440
as the tool, as well
as additional tools,

00:22:36.440 --> 00:22:38.940
in a future episode about task
switching on the Huberman Lab

00:22:38.940 --> 00:22:41.780
podcast.

00:22:41.780 --> 00:22:45.440
If you were to, for
instance, close your eyes

00:22:45.440 --> 00:22:48.590
and not look at anything in
your external environment

00:22:48.590 --> 00:22:50.510
and just concentrate,
for instance,

00:22:50.510 --> 00:22:53.310
on your breathing or the feeling
on the surface of your skin--

00:22:53.310 --> 00:22:55.935
I know this is starting to sound
like meditation, but trust me,

00:22:55.935 --> 00:22:57.590
it's not meditation--

00:22:57.590 --> 00:23:00.290
your perception of time,
that is, how finely you

00:23:00.290 --> 00:23:04.070
are slicing time, would
be distinctly different

00:23:04.070 --> 00:23:06.290
than if you were
to open your eyes

00:23:06.290 --> 00:23:11.070
and focus on a faraway location,
say way off in the horizon,

00:23:11.070 --> 00:23:15.150
and not focus on your
bodily sensations.

00:23:15.150 --> 00:23:17.610
Similarly, if you were
to focus your attention

00:23:17.610 --> 00:23:20.310
on some intermediate
location, maybe,

00:23:20.310 --> 00:23:24.670
let's say, 20 feet away
and simultaneously,

00:23:24.670 --> 00:23:27.370
focus on your internal
bodily sensations

00:23:27.370 --> 00:23:31.310
or the surface of your skin,
your perception of time how

00:23:31.310 --> 00:23:34.280
quickly time was
passing would also

00:23:34.280 --> 00:23:36.800
be different than if
you closed your eyes

00:23:36.800 --> 00:23:39.410
or if you were looking
at some distant location.

00:23:39.410 --> 00:23:42.680
The perceptual tool for task
switching is a very simple one.

00:23:42.680 --> 00:23:44.480
It's one that frankly,
I do every morning

00:23:44.480 --> 00:23:47.690
and have for many years now and
at least, for me, has really

00:23:47.690 --> 00:23:50.060
enhanced my ability
to task switch.

00:23:50.060 --> 00:23:52.420
That is, just take
a couple of minutes.

00:23:52.420 --> 00:23:54.980
This really only takes
about two or three minutes.

00:23:54.980 --> 00:23:58.118
Typically, what I will do is,
I'll start by closing my eyes.

00:23:58.118 --> 00:23:59.660
I should mention,
I typically do this

00:23:59.660 --> 00:24:01.610
in an environment
where ideally, I

00:24:01.610 --> 00:24:04.580
can see off into the distance,
perhaps from a balcony

00:24:04.580 --> 00:24:08.205
if I'm in an apartment or
a house, ideally outdoors.

00:24:08.205 --> 00:24:09.830
But if I'm indoors,
I'll still do this.

00:24:09.830 --> 00:24:12.050
I'll just look as far
off into the distance

00:24:12.050 --> 00:24:15.380
as I can when that
step is required.

00:24:15.380 --> 00:24:18.410
I start off by closing my eyes
and essentially not looking

00:24:18.410 --> 00:24:20.810
at anything, but
directing my brain's focus

00:24:20.810 --> 00:24:22.220
to either the
surface of my body,

00:24:22.220 --> 00:24:24.595
just what it feels like, what
it's in contact with or not

00:24:24.595 --> 00:24:26.630
in contact with,
maybe my breathing.

00:24:26.630 --> 00:24:28.190
Then, I'll open
my eyes and I will

00:24:28.190 --> 00:24:31.670
focus on some location on my
body but my bodily surface,

00:24:31.670 --> 00:24:34.530
like my hand at some distance.

00:24:34.530 --> 00:24:36.450
I'll focus my
attention there maybe

00:24:36.450 --> 00:24:38.770
for just five to 15 seconds.

00:24:38.770 --> 00:24:40.992
I should mention that
the first station,

00:24:40.992 --> 00:24:42.700
as I call them, where
my eyes were closed

00:24:42.700 --> 00:24:44.130
and I was focusing on
my bodily sensations,

00:24:44.130 --> 00:24:46.350
I also just do that for
about five to 15 seconds.

00:24:46.350 --> 00:24:52.170
I don't count specifically,
just roughly five to 15 seconds.

00:24:52.170 --> 00:24:55.560
Second station, you're looking
at the surface of your hand.

00:24:55.560 --> 00:24:58.110
If you like, you can also
concentrate on your breathing.

00:24:58.110 --> 00:25:01.697
But typically, people will just
focus on some specific location

00:25:01.697 --> 00:25:02.280
on their hand.

00:25:02.280 --> 00:25:04.020
Then, I'll typically
lower my hand.

00:25:04.020 --> 00:25:05.880
Then, I'll look off
into the distance, maybe

00:25:05.880 --> 00:25:06.660
five to 10 feet.

00:25:06.660 --> 00:25:07.840
It doesn't really matter.

00:25:07.840 --> 00:25:09.510
Focus my visual attention there.

00:25:09.510 --> 00:25:11.880
Try and hold that focus
for five to 15 seconds.

00:25:11.880 --> 00:25:15.750
Then, I'll look further off in
the distance, maybe, further

00:25:15.750 --> 00:25:17.640
still off into the distance.

00:25:17.640 --> 00:25:19.860
Ultimately, what I try and
do is look at a location

00:25:19.860 --> 00:25:25.140
as far off into the
distance as I possibly can.

00:25:25.140 --> 00:25:27.450
I'm also trying to pay
attention to my breathing

00:25:27.450 --> 00:25:29.910
at the same time, just
as a way of calibrating

00:25:29.910 --> 00:25:33.430
my location to the location
that I'm looking at

00:25:33.430 --> 00:25:34.653
and how great that is.

00:25:34.653 --> 00:25:36.070
Then typically,
I'll close my eyes

00:25:36.070 --> 00:25:38.690
and return my attention to
my immediate environment

00:25:38.690 --> 00:25:41.620
and my breathing just
in the location I'm in.

00:25:41.620 --> 00:25:44.050
The entire thing only
takes about two minutes.

00:25:44.050 --> 00:25:47.210
Again, starting with eyes
closed, focusing on self five

00:25:47.210 --> 00:25:48.040
to 15 seconds.

00:25:48.040 --> 00:25:52.030
Then, eyes open, focusing on
surface of one's body, that is,

00:25:52.030 --> 00:25:54.820
focusing one's visual attention
five to 15 seconds maybe

00:25:54.820 --> 00:25:58.540
10 feet away, then
maybe, 50 feet away.

00:25:58.540 --> 00:26:01.880
If you're in the metric system,
meters works just as well.

00:26:01.880 --> 00:26:04.000
These distances do not
have to be precise.

00:26:04.000 --> 00:26:07.240
And then, off to the
horizon and then,

00:26:07.240 --> 00:26:10.390
back to one's immediate
location by closing one's eyes.

00:26:10.390 --> 00:26:14.050
What is happening when one
does this perceptual exercise?

00:26:14.050 --> 00:26:15.580
Again, it's a
perceptual exercise.

00:26:15.580 --> 00:26:18.220
It's a visual
perceptual exercise.

00:26:18.220 --> 00:26:20.800
What's happening
is, you are shifting

00:26:20.800 --> 00:26:23.170
your visual focus, obviously.

00:26:23.170 --> 00:26:26.320
But you're also shifting
the way in which you

00:26:26.320 --> 00:26:29.860
fine slice or thick slice time.

00:26:29.860 --> 00:26:32.330
Your ability to recognize,
consciously, whether or not

00:26:32.330 --> 00:26:36.020
your thin slicing or thick
slicing time is much harder

00:26:36.020 --> 00:26:39.243
to get a grasp of than it
is to get a grasp of whether

00:26:39.243 --> 00:26:41.660
or not you're looking at your
hand or off in the distance.

00:26:41.660 --> 00:26:42.680
That's kind of obvious.

00:26:42.680 --> 00:26:44.210
But what we know for
sure is that as you

00:26:44.210 --> 00:26:46.377
shift your attention from
your immediate environment

00:26:46.377 --> 00:26:49.160
out to different designated
locations in your environment,

00:26:49.160 --> 00:26:52.250
and your time perception
shifts accordingly,

00:26:52.250 --> 00:26:54.140
you're essentially
training your brain

00:26:54.140 --> 00:26:56.990
to shift visual focus
and the way in which you

00:26:56.990 --> 00:26:59.300
process in the time domain.

00:26:59.300 --> 00:27:01.460
This is important in the
context of task switching,

00:27:01.460 --> 00:27:03.590
because so much
of task switching

00:27:03.590 --> 00:27:07.130
is not just to
understand OK, I'm

00:27:07.130 --> 00:27:11.678
going from reading to running
or from running to reading

00:27:11.678 --> 00:27:13.970
and the different types of
operations that are required

00:27:13.970 --> 00:27:16.910
in one case versus
the other, but also,

00:27:16.910 --> 00:27:20.390
a shift in the neural
circuits that underlie

00:27:20.390 --> 00:27:22.520
your perception of time.

00:27:22.520 --> 00:27:24.800
Again, this is a topic
that deserves a much more

00:27:24.800 --> 00:27:26.660
elaborate discussion.

00:27:26.660 --> 00:27:30.710
So much of our ability
to execute a task

00:27:30.710 --> 00:27:33.350
with high proficiency
has to do with getting

00:27:33.350 --> 00:27:37.878
our thinking and our actions
into the correct time domain.

00:27:37.878 --> 00:27:39.920
When I say time domain,
I know a number of people

00:27:39.920 --> 00:27:42.620
can get confused
because time is time.

00:27:42.620 --> 00:27:44.690
People think, what do
you mean by time domain?

00:27:44.690 --> 00:27:46.063
Space domain makes sense.

00:27:46.063 --> 00:27:47.730
Here, I'm not talking
about outer space,

00:27:47.730 --> 00:27:51.105
whether or not you're looking
in one location or another close

00:27:51.105 --> 00:27:52.730
to your body or far
away from your body

00:27:52.730 --> 00:27:55.520
are different domains of space.

00:27:55.520 --> 00:27:58.357
But the time domain is a
little trickier for most people

00:27:58.357 --> 00:27:58.940
to understand.

00:27:58.940 --> 00:28:00.210
Just think of it this way.

00:28:00.210 --> 00:28:03.560
When you see a slow motion
movie, what you're seeing

00:28:03.560 --> 00:28:05.600
is a movie that was
shot at a high frame

00:28:05.600 --> 00:28:08.240
rate, many frames per second.

00:28:08.240 --> 00:28:09.890
The typical smartphone
shoots movies

00:28:09.890 --> 00:28:12.170
at about 60 frames per
second, some older ones,

00:28:12.170 --> 00:28:13.190
30 frames per second.

00:28:13.190 --> 00:28:17.300
The slow-mo function
on your smartphone

00:28:17.300 --> 00:28:20.690
is actually a high
frame rate function.

00:28:20.690 --> 00:28:24.620
You took the same movie, but you
took it at a higher frame rate.

00:28:24.620 --> 00:28:26.250
You've got a lot more images.

00:28:26.250 --> 00:28:28.615
Therefore, you can
generate slow motion.

00:28:28.615 --> 00:28:29.990
With your visual
system, when you

00:28:29.990 --> 00:28:32.630
focus very close in
to your body or you're

00:28:32.630 --> 00:28:36.590
focused on bodily sensations
in your immediate environment,

00:28:36.590 --> 00:28:39.470
you are fine slicing
in the time domain

00:28:39.470 --> 00:28:42.470
more so than when
you are looking

00:28:42.470 --> 00:28:44.360
further off in the distance.

00:28:44.360 --> 00:28:46.790
Similarly, when you engage
in one type of task,

00:28:46.790 --> 00:28:48.530
like a board meeting
or a Zoom meeting

00:28:48.530 --> 00:28:50.660
or a conversation
with friends, you

00:28:50.660 --> 00:28:54.320
are in a very different set
of neural circuit functions

00:28:54.320 --> 00:28:58.190
than when you sit down to read
or learn math or lift weights

00:28:58.190 --> 00:29:02.310
or go to therapy or go
for a walk with your dog,

00:29:02.310 --> 00:29:03.950
for instance.

00:29:03.950 --> 00:29:06.800
Now, it should be clear why
when you move from task to task

00:29:06.800 --> 00:29:11.090
you want to A, introduce
a transition period.

00:29:11.090 --> 00:29:11.998
It can be very brief.

00:29:11.998 --> 00:29:14.540
Maybe, you don't even have time
for the two minute transition

00:29:14.540 --> 00:29:14.910
period.

00:29:14.910 --> 00:29:15.480
You just say, OK.

00:29:15.480 --> 00:29:17.600
I'm in a transition period
between task A and task

00:29:17.600 --> 00:29:19.610
B. I'm moving from this
thing to that thing.

00:29:19.610 --> 00:29:21.172
I just need like 10 seconds.

00:29:21.172 --> 00:29:22.130
I'm going to recognize.

00:29:22.130 --> 00:29:25.650
I'm going to count down
10 to one or one up to 10.

00:29:25.650 --> 00:29:26.450
It doesn't matter.

00:29:26.450 --> 00:29:27.740
This is transition time.

00:29:27.740 --> 00:29:31.160
But this is not a time
to look at my phone

00:29:31.160 --> 00:29:33.890
or to be in lots of
different time domains.

00:29:33.890 --> 00:29:36.802
You might say,
well, does that mean

00:29:36.802 --> 00:29:38.510
I shouldn't look at
the horizon while I'm

00:29:38.510 --> 00:29:40.250
walking from my meeting
back to my desk?

00:29:40.250 --> 00:29:41.182
No.

00:29:41.182 --> 00:29:42.890
That's not the way
that your brain works.

00:29:42.890 --> 00:29:44.873
It doesn't anchor to
things that just happen

00:29:44.873 --> 00:29:47.540
to be in your environment unless
they're of particular interest.

00:29:47.540 --> 00:29:50.790
What I'm saying is, set a
transition period between tasks

00:29:50.790 --> 00:29:54.690
ideally, two maybe as
long as 10 minutes.

00:29:54.690 --> 00:29:58.010
I'm also saying that when
you switch between tasks,

00:29:58.010 --> 00:30:00.770
or when you initiate your
first major task of the day,

00:30:00.770 --> 00:30:02.150
please expect--

00:30:02.150 --> 00:30:05.810
do expect-- a
period in which it's

00:30:05.810 --> 00:30:09.620
hard to get into the
groove, so to speak.

00:30:09.620 --> 00:30:12.883
In addition to that, I recommend
having some of practice.

00:30:12.883 --> 00:30:14.300
I described the
practice that I've

00:30:14.300 --> 00:30:16.910
used for some period of
time now, at least for me

00:30:16.910 --> 00:30:19.880
to great success, where
you are deliberately

00:30:19.880 --> 00:30:22.970
shifting your visual attention
between different locations

00:30:22.970 --> 00:30:24.350
close to you and far away.

00:30:24.350 --> 00:30:26.750
You're doing that as
a perceptual practice.

00:30:26.750 --> 00:30:29.000
Again, the whole thing only
takes about two minutes,

00:30:29.000 --> 00:30:30.295
maybe three minutes.

00:30:30.295 --> 00:30:31.920
You don't even need
to do it every day.

00:30:31.920 --> 00:30:34.010
I happen to do it every day,
but I miss the occasional day

00:30:34.010 --> 00:30:34.880
here and there.

00:30:34.880 --> 00:30:39.590
Even if you were to do this
perceptual practice once a week

00:30:39.590 --> 00:30:41.990
or three times a week, I'm
certain that you'll benefit.

00:30:41.990 --> 00:30:43.890
Because in doing that
perceptual practice,

00:30:43.890 --> 00:30:46.160
there's also an
immediate recognition

00:30:46.160 --> 00:30:48.350
of the sorts of shifts
that your brain is required

00:30:48.350 --> 00:30:51.110
to engage in any time you
move from task A to task B

00:30:51.110 --> 00:30:53.810
or from task B to
task C. You start

00:30:53.810 --> 00:30:58.070
to see and feel,
literally see and feel,

00:30:58.070 --> 00:31:00.840
the way that transition occurs.

00:31:00.840 --> 00:31:02.090
It takes a little bit of time.

00:31:02.090 --> 00:31:04.290
But you can accelerate
that transition

00:31:04.290 --> 00:31:07.310
if you understand that oh, when
I'm looking here and engaging

00:31:07.310 --> 00:31:10.550
in this type of behavior
or sets of tasks

00:31:10.550 --> 00:31:12.560
and then, I'm now
going to be expected

00:31:12.560 --> 00:31:14.480
to do another task
in a completely

00:31:14.480 --> 00:31:18.200
different type of environment,
the brain is going

00:31:18.200 --> 00:31:20.690
to be required to shift over
the neural circuits that

00:31:20.690 --> 00:31:23.540
are active and less active
in order to do that.

00:31:23.540 --> 00:31:25.820
You can accelerate that
process by practicing

00:31:25.820 --> 00:31:28.850
it using that perceptual
tool that I described.

00:31:28.850 --> 00:31:30.360
There, I covered
some specific tools

00:31:30.360 --> 00:31:33.300
that one can use to enhance
one's ability to task switch,

00:31:33.300 --> 00:31:35.700
touching on a bit of the
underlying neurobiology

00:31:35.700 --> 00:31:39.570
and why transition periods
are useful, if not required.

00:31:39.570 --> 00:31:42.055
If you think about there's
always a transition period when

00:31:42.055 --> 00:31:44.430
task switching, but here,
you're taking conscious control

00:31:44.430 --> 00:31:45.870
over that transition period.

00:31:45.870 --> 00:31:48.630
There are additional tools
for enhancing one's ability

00:31:48.630 --> 00:31:49.530
to task switch.

00:31:49.530 --> 00:31:53.280
They tend to be somewhat
specific for the certain kinds

00:31:53.280 --> 00:31:56.730
of cognitive or physical
tasks that one needs to do.

00:31:56.730 --> 00:31:59.160
The example of chess
boxing that I gave earlier,

00:31:59.160 --> 00:32:00.630
a great example
of task switching

00:32:00.630 --> 00:32:03.120
at its extreme, terrible
example of a practice

00:32:03.120 --> 00:32:04.252
space-time bridging.

00:32:04.252 --> 00:32:06.210
Very safe, I can't think
of any way in which it

00:32:06.210 --> 00:32:08.168
might be dangerous,
although please don't do it

00:32:08.168 --> 00:32:12.030
while driving or while
operating any other machinery.

00:32:12.030 --> 00:32:13.795
By all accounts,
very safe, zero cost.

00:32:13.795 --> 00:32:15.420
We talked about some
of the other tools

00:32:15.420 --> 00:32:17.290
for task switching as well.

